From 57a138edb3e2603f5df588df40279a42de450599 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 16:08:58 -0300
Subject: [PATCH v2 10/13] hv: vmbus: pin buffer pages across UIO mmap to close
 the reclaim race

UIO maps the channel ring, the send/receive buffers, the interrupt
page and the monitor page directly into userspace. Those maps are
built from page arrays that the reclaim worker can free once the
buffer owner is released: the snapshot taken in the mmap path and
the moment the new VMA holds its own references are not the same
instant, so a reclaim in that window frees pages a mapping is about
to publish.

Add vmbus_buffer_pin_pages() and vmbus_buffer_unpin_pages(). Pinning
takes a reference on every page of a vmbus_buffer and refuses to
start once reclaiming has begun or the owner leaked for good. The
owner and page-array snapshot is taken under vmbus_buffer_owners_lock,
the lock that publishes and clears both fields.

The pin cannot be dropped when mmap_prepare() returns.
mmap_action_map_kernel_pages() only stores the page-array pointer, and
the mapping takes its own folio references inside insert_pages(),
which runs after the hook. Dropping the pin therefore waits for the
VMA to be established (vm_ops->mapped) or for the attempt to be
abandoned (vm_ops->close). Release is one-shot: both can run.

close() has to be reachable on every failure path. The legacy .mmap
error path frees the VMA without calling vma_close(), and a failure
in mmap_action_prepare() never reaches compat_set_vma_from_desc().
Both call sites that drive a driver-owned pin - the UIO core mmap
hook and the ring-buffer sysfs wrapper - therefore install desc.vm_ops
and desc.private_data onto the VMA before __compat_vma_mmap() and
call close() when it fails. Drivers that leave vm_ops alone keep
their existing vma_private_data, and close() is safe without a prior
mapped() and tolerates a private_data this driver did not install.

The ring-buffer sysfs mmap and the UIO mmap_prepare hook share one
page-selection and validation path. The interrupt and monitor pages
are snapshot as struct page * at probe, where the backing is
guaranteed to still exist, and are not buffer-backed so they take no
pin. The six KUnit cases cover the
range check, the ring mmap path, the per-map region selection, the
mmap_prepare hook, the pin spanning the mapped() boundary, and
close() before mapped() - including a second close and a foreign
private_data. They are included from uio_hv_generic.c so they can
reach the static helpers without exporting them.

Signed-off-by: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
